  "query_preview": "periods of time outdoors will need to remain adequately \nhydrated and take frequent breaks in the shade or air conditioning \nin order to avoid developing heat-related illness. Lows continue in \nthe 70s with conditions muggy.\n\nDeep moisture (PWATs well above 2\") surge across ECFL. A weak \npressure gradient remains in place with associated ridge axis \nsouth/east of the area and light W/NW flow forecast. The ECSB will \ndevelop but it will be delayed with slower movement inland during \nthe afternoon. The offshore flow remains fairly deep, but very \nlight. There will be a weakness in mid-level ridging across the \nregion with NW/WNW flow overhead. It remains fairly warm aloft with \n500 mb temps at -4.5C to -6.0C, though there will be occasional weak \nshortwave impulses embedded in the flow that will aid diurnal \nconvection. Initial convection will develop near the east coast by \nlate morning and early afternoon with coverage and intensity \nincreasing across the eastern FL peninsula by late day as stronger \nboundary collisions occur. Storm steering likely light NWRLY at 5-10 \nmph as local CAMs show convection \"zippering\" southward along the \nsea breeze boundary, though slow movement and multiple rounds for \nsome could increase precip totals locally (2-5\") with minor/nuisance \nflooding resulting. Continue to cap PoPs at 70% areawide and the \nWeather Prediction Center (WPC) keeps much of the eastern FL \npeninsula in a Marginal (5-14%) Excessive Rainfall Outlook. \n\nIn addition to a heavy rainfall threat, The Storm Prediction Center \n(SPC) has placed Volusia, Lake, Seminole, Orange, and north Brevard \ncounties under a Marginal Risk for Severe Storms today. Lightning \nstrikes and gusty winds 50-60 mph locally will also be possible with \nstronger convection. Activity will gradually diminishing across the \npeninsula through the mid-late evening.\n\nMon-Sat...Persistent pattern developing for the week ahead as the \nAtlc ridge axis remains south of the area thru the extended. The \noffshore flow at 925 mb strengthens from Mon onward and the ECSB \nwill be hard-pressed to develop Mon/Tue (if at all) with very \nlimited movement inland, then not likely over the remainder of the \nperiod. PWAT values remain stout and nearly 2\" or above each day. A \nfrontal boundary is still forecast to drop towards north FL mid-week \nand remaining quasi-stationary into late week well north of ECFL. \nThe pattern aloft will remain rather \"troughy\" along the Atlc \nSeaboard extending south into the FL peninsula, thus allowing \nperiodic shortwave features to dive across the area enhancing \ndiurnal convection daily. SCT-NMRS (30-70%) PoPs (aftn/eve) each \nday. Main focus will be on heavy rainfall potential on top of \nfrequent lightning strikes and gusty winds with storms. \n\nNo real break in sight with high temps (90-96F) and peak heat \nindices continuing to average 102-107F each afternoon ahead of sea \nbreeze/storms. Will continue to monitor for the need of any Heat \nAdvisories, as portions of ECFL could still see heat index values \nreach around 108-110F. Heat safety will remain key for residents and \nvisitors. Adequate hydration, frequent breaks in the shade or air \nconditioning, and dressing in lightweight, lightly colored clothing \nif spending extended periods of time outdoors will help reduce the \nchance of developing heat illness for the upcoming week.",
